<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Adolf II, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–<a href="/years/1746">1746</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Adolf II was the last duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, ruling from 1736 to 1746. A field marshal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army, he commanded Saxon forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> and was defeated by the Prussians at Hohenfriedberg in 1745. On his death the duchy reverted to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-adolf-ii-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels.jpg" alt="Johann Adolf II, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Pesne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antoine_Pesne_(1683-1757)_-_John_Adolphus_II,_Duke_of_Saxe-Weissenfels_(1685-1746)_-_RCIN_405656_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Johann Adolf was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a> on 19 August 1685, the third surviving son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-adolf-i-duke-of-saxe-weissenfels">Johann Adolf I, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, and his first wife, Johanna Magdalena of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>. As a younger son he made his career as a commander in the Saxon Army. He inherited the duchy in 1736, when his older brother Christian died without children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1733, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>, Johann Adolf led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The Saxon army stayed mainly in southern Poland for the next three years, until Elector Frederick August II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was crowned King of Poland after the defeat of the rival candidate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a>.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-silesian-war">Second Silesian War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> agreed to act together against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops planning to cut off the Prussians in northern Silesia while Austrian troops advanced from the south. In June 1745 they were defeated at the Battle of Hohenfriedberg.</p><p>Johann Adolf planned a new offensive in September but abandoned it two weeks later, and he was replaced as commander-in-chief by Count Frederick August Rutowski, an illegitimate half-brother of the King-Elector. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kesselsdorf">Battle of Kesselsdorf</a> the Elector removed Rutowski and reinstated Johann Adolf, who took up the command again on 1 December 1745 and was also made head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> government while the Elector and the minister Heinrich of Brühl were absent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Johann Adolf retreated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and died of a heart attack on 14 May 1746, at the age of sixty-one. He had married Johannette Antoinette Juliane of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a> in 1721 and Fredericka of Saxe-Gotha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> in 1734, and of his six children only a daughter, Fredericka Adolfine, survived infancy; she died in 1751, still a child. He was the last of the line, and his lands returned to the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, from which they had been separated in 1657 under the will of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">John George I, Elector of Saxony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johann Adolf II of Saxe-Weissenfels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Johann Adolf II</b>, the last duke and a Saxon field marshal, died in 1746, and the duchy reverted to the Electorate of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weissenfels">Duchy of Saxe-Weissenfels</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weißenfels</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.2,11.97&amp;year=1724" aria-label="Weißenfels on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4822 390 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johann Adolf II, Duke of Saxe-Weissenfels"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.2,11.97&amp;year=1724">What was here in 1724? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
